---@class Sprite
Sprite = {}

--- The sheet's index in the scene, or -1 when no sheet has that name.
---
--- `Sprite.SheetIndex(name)`
---@param name any
---@return any
function Sprite.SheetIndex(name) end

--- The animation's index, or -1 when none has that name. It searches every sheet and returns the
--- first match, which is why the editor makes animation names unique across the scene.
---
--- `Sprite.AnimIndex(name)`
---@param name any
---@return any
function Sprite.AnimIndex(name) end

--- Makes a sprite from a sheet and returns its id, 0 to 127. Returns -1 when the sheet does not
--- exist or all 128 are in use. A new sprite is visible, on the screen plane at (0, 0), on layer 0,
--- with a neutral tint, showing cell 0 and no animation.
---
--- `Sprite.Create(sheetName)`
---@param sheetName any
---@return any
function Sprite.Create(sheetName) end

--- Frees the slot. The id may be handed out again by the next Create.
---
--- `Sprite.Destroy(id)`
---@param id any
---@return any
function Sprite.Destroy(id) end

--- Screen position of the sprite's top left corner, in whole pixels (-32768 to 32767).
---
--- `Sprite.SetPos(id, x, y)`
---@param id any
---@param x any
---@param y any
---@return any
function Sprite.SetPos(id, x, y) end

--- Position on the world plane. The engine keeps it but does not draw world-plane sprites yet.
---
--- `Sprite.SetWorldPos(id, x, y, z)`
---@param id any
---@param x any
---@param y any
---@param z any
---@return any
function Sprite.SetWorldPos(id, x, y, z) end

--- Makes the sprite follow an object every frame. actor is an Actor.Find or Entity.Find handle; nil
--- unbinds it. plane is 0 for the screen plane (the default) or 1 for the world plane; the offsets
--- default to 0. On the screen plane the sprite's x is the object's X and its y the object's Z, in
--- whole engine units, plus the offsets; the object's height is ignored. The bound position
--- replaces whatever SetPos set.
---
--- `Sprite.BindToActor(id, actor, plane, offsetX, offsetY)`
---@param id any
---@param actor any
---@param plane any
---@param offsetX any
---@param offsetY any
---@return any
function Sprite.BindToActor(id, actor, plane, offsetX, offsetY) end

--- Shows one cell of the sprite's sheet, 0 to 255, counted row by row from the top left, and drops
--- the animation. The cell is not checked against the sheet's size.
---
--- `Sprite.SetFrame(id, cell)`
---@param id any
---@param cell any
---@return any
function Sprite.SetFrame(id, cell) end

--- Plays an animation; the sprite switches to the animation's sheet. If the same animation is
--- already playing it carries on, so calling this every frame is fine; pass true as restart to
--- start it again from its first cell. An unknown name does nothing. Each cell shows for the
--- animation's frame time in vsyncs, 60 a second of real time. A looping animation starts again
--- after its last cell; otherwise it stops there.
---
--- `Sprite.PlayAnim(id, animName, restart)`
---@param id any
---@param animName any
---@param restart any
---@return any
function Sprite.PlayAnim(id, animName, restart) end

--- Stops the animation on the cell it is showing.
---
--- `Sprite.StopAnim(id)`
---@param id any
---@return any
function Sprite.StopAnim(id) end

--- For a character drawn from several directions: plays animation firstAnimName + n, where n (0 to
--- directions - 1) is the direction nearest to yaw. The directional animations must follow one
--- another in the sheet's list, starting with yaw 0 and going the way yaw increases. yaw is in
--- pi-units like Entity.SetRotationY (1 = 180 degrees). Changing direction keeps the cell and
--- timing, so a walk cycle does not restart, and keeps whether it is playing: call PlayAnim first.
--- directions of 0 does nothing.
---
--- `Sprite.SetFacingFromYaw(id, firstAnimName, directions, yaw)`
---@param id any
---@param firstAnimName any
---@param directions any
---@param yaw any
---@return any
function Sprite.SetFacingFromYaw(id, firstAnimName, directions, yaw) end

